<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Yelang</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 27 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest kingdom of the south-western peoples in Guizhou, remembered for a king who asked a Han envoy whether Han was larger than Yelang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yelang was a polity of the south-western peoples in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, emerging in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. It controlled the Zangke River route down to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> and the lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> describe Yelang as a settled kingdom of farmers with walled towns. Han envoys reached it in 135 BCE seeking a route to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a>, and the marquis Duotong accepted Han titles. Its ruler's question to the envoys about which state was larger gave rise to the Chinese idiom for self-importance born of ignorance.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanyue">Nanyue</a> in 111 BCE, Yelang submitted and its ruler received the title of king, while Han established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangke-commandery">Zangke Commandery</a>. Around 27 BCE the Han governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-li-han-dynasty">Chen Li</a> executed the king, Xing, for rebellion and the kingdom was abolished.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 300 BCE – 27 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:53.73%;width:5.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 27 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duotong, Xing</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yelang Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-164&amp;focus=yelang" aria-label="Yelang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7529 1168 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yelang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7849 1424L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7849 1424h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7849 1424h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-164&amp;focus=yelang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

